  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-54x-30 \\\\  & =6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5)<0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6(2x+1)(x-5)<0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=6(2x+1)(x-5)$ has roots at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/7eec3fc84b851b3b39e5ae8ecff1343f39393d1dd8f7a6cce8e8904dc1c65d48.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5)$ between the roots, so we conclude that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$",
